{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Because of his young age a regency was established with his mother Hodierna and later his uncle Joscelin II of Edessa playing key roles. Raymond spent much of his youth under the protection of powerful nobles and the Church. His guardianship was contested by several factions within the kingdom of Jerusalem which made his early rule fragile and uncertain.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became an influential figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close ally and later a bitter rival of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was named regent of Jerusalem during Baldwin IVs minority and again during his illness. He often clashed with other nobles especially the Knights Templar and the Hospitallers. His leadership was criticized by some for being cautious and indecisive. He was also involved in multiple diplomatic missions to Muslim rulers seeking peace or alliances. His negotiations with Nur ad-Din and later Saladin were seen as pragmatic but also as signs of weakness by some of his peers. Raymond was known for his deep knowledge of Arabic and his ability to communicate directly with Muslim leaders which gave him a unique advantage in diplomacy.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the disastrous Battle of Hattin. He was present at the battle and survived the initial defeat but was captured by Saladin. He was held prisoner for a short time and then released. His health had been failing for years and he died soon after his release. His death marked the end of a long and turbulent chapter in the history of the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond IIIs legacy is mixed. He was a skilled diplomat who understood the complexities of the region but his cautious approach sometimes alienated his allies. He was a key player in the politics of the Levant during one of its most volatile periods. His life reflected the fragile balance between war and negotiation that defined the Crusader states. His story is a testament to the personal and political challenges faced by the nobility in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -4.625213477993384e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.002193308901041746}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.005234109237790108}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.775113539944869e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.007520932704210281}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.005743073765188456}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.08055154979228973}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -8.821448318485636e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009454786195419729}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00020180096908006817}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.611976353568025e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.38688841462135315}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.003029045881703496}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.5735502529423684e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob